```

If a destroy hook throws while the platform is being destroyed, the error escapes the `setTimeout` in `asyncDestroyPlatform()` and ends the process on servers that use `AngularAppEngine`. #34768 added a `try/finally` there, but that still lets the error through.

It doesn't matter whether the component is routed or not. A component that creates a subscription once an HTTP call answers ends the process if the visitor leaves before the answer:

```ts
export class Profile implements OnInit, OnDestroy {
  private readonly http = inject(HttpClient);
  private readonly updates = new Subject<string>();
  private subscription!: Subscription;
  protected readonly name = signal('');

  ngOnInit() {
    this.http.get<{ name: string }>('http://localhost:4400/api/profile').subscribe(({ name }) => {
      this.name.set(name);
      this.subscription = this.updates.subscribe((next) => this.name.set(next));
    });
  }

  ngOnDestroy() {
    // undefined if the visitor left before the answer
    this.subscription.unsubscribe();
  }
}
```

A routed component behind an HTTP resolver only needs a plain subscribe. In a zoneless app, if the request is aborted right as the resolver answers, the component is destroyed before its first change detection and `ngOnDestroy` runs without `ngOnInit`:

```ts
export const routes: Routes = [
  {
    path: '',
    component: Dashboard,
    resolve: { user: () => inject(HttpClient).get('http://localhost:4400/api/user') },
  },
];

export class Dashboard implements OnInit, OnDestroy {
  private readonly notifications = inject(Notifications);
  private subscription!: Subscription;
  protected readonly unread = signal(0);

  ngOnInit() {
    this.subscription = this.notifications.unread$.subscribe((n) => this.unread.set(n));
  }

  ngOnDestroy() {
    // can run before ngOnInit
    this.subscription.unsubscribe();
  }
}
```

The error is now caught and logged. angular/angular#72139 also catches it in the `@angular/platform-server` copy. Here it is logged instead of rejected, since nothing awaits the destroy.

Related to #34754

`oxc-transform` previously only elided `ɵsetClassMetadata` / `ɵsetClassMetadataAsync` / `ɵsetClassDebugInfo` calls inside `FunctionExpression` / `ArrowFunctionExpression` IIFEs, and `analyzeClassStaticProperties()` set `shouldWrap = false` whenever a class contained any `StaticBlock` other than `this.<prop> = <expr>`.

When full-mode Angular classes emit metadata inside an ES2022 `static { ... }` block (as `ngp` already does, and as proposed for `ngtsc` in angular/angular#72136 — including `@angular/core/fesm2022/core.mjs`, where Rollup bundles internal `setClassMetadata` calls without the `ɵ` prefix), `oxc-transform` previously left the `static {}` block intact and skipped wrapping the class in a `/*#__PURE__*/` IIFE, preventing unused classes such as `PlatformRef` and `ApplicationModule` from being tree-shaken (+10.0 kB in standalone apps).

This updates `oxc-transform` and `traversal` to:
- Recognize `ɵsetClassMetadata*` calls and `ngDevMode`/`ngJitMode`-guarded `(ɵ)setClassMetadata*` statements inside `StaticBlock`s, removing metadata-only `StaticBlock`s (and individual metadata statements inside multi-statement `StaticBlock`s) when `advancedOptimizations` is enabled.
- Allow `analyzeClassStaticProperties()` to continue wrapping classes whose `StaticBlock`s contain only elidable Angular metadata and/or pure `this.<prop> = <expr>` assignments, while still leaving user side-effectful `StaticBlock`s unwrapped.

### Minimal failure example

**Input (`@angular/core` / `ngp` / `ngtsc` ES2022 output):**
```js
export class PlatformRef {
  static ɵfac = function PlatformRef_Factory(t) { return new (t || PlatformRef)(); };
  static ɵprov = /*@__PURE__*/ ɵɵdefineInjectable({ token: PlatformRef, factory: PlatformRef.ɵfac, providedIn: 'platform' });
  static {
    (typeof ngDevMode === "undefined" || ngDevMode) &&
      setClassMetadata(PlatformRef, [{ type: Injectable, args: [{ providedIn: 'platform' }] }], null, null);
  }
}
```

**Before (`main`):**
Left unchanged (`shouldWrap = false`), preventing `PlatformRef` from being wrapped in `/*#__PURE__*/ (() => { ... })()` and retaining it in standalone application bundles.

**After:**
```js
export let PlatformRef = /*#__PURE__*/ (() => {
  class PlatformRef {
    static ɵfac = function PlatformRef_Factory(t) { return new (t || PlatformRef)(); };
    static ɵprov = /*@__PURE__*/ ɵɵdefineInjectable({ token: PlatformRef, factory: PlatformRef.ɵfac, providedIn: 'platform' });
  }
  return PlatformRef;
})();
```

### Which @angular/* package(s) are the source of the bug?

@ngtools/webpack (via @angular-devkit/build-angular, `browser` builder)

### Is this a regression?

Yes. The last version that works is @angular-devkit/build-angular 22.1.9. It fails from 22.2.0 through 22.3.0 (the latest at the time of writing), whatever the @angular/* framework version: build-angular 22.1.9 with Angular 22.2.0 works, and build-angular 22.2.0 with Angular 22.1.8 fails.

### Description

When one import statement brings in a `const enum` together with a runtime value that is used only in type positions (for example a class that is only a constructor parameter type), the webpack build fails:

```
./src/app/app.component.ts:1:0-34 - Error: export 'Period' (imported as 'Period') was not found in './period' (possible exports: PeriodFormatter)
```

```ts
// period.ts
import { Injectable } from '@angular/core';

export const enum Period {
	Month = 'month',
	Quarter = 'quarter',
}

@Injectable({ providedIn: 'root' })
export class PeriodFormatter {
	format(period: string): string {
		return `Period: ${period}`;
	}
}

// app.component.ts
import { Component } from '@angular/core';
import { Period, PeriodFormatter } from './period';

@Component({ selector: 'app-root', template: '<p>{{ label }}</p>' })
export class AppComponent {
	protected readonly label: string;

	constructor(formatter: PeriodFormatter) {
		this.label = formatter.format(Period.Month);
	}
}
```

What seems to happen, judging from `elide_imports.ts`: `PeriodFormatter` is only referenced from a type node, so its specifier is classed as an unused runtime value and the declaration is rewritten without it. The comment there notes that TypeScript skips its own import elision for a declaration a transformer replaced. `Period` counts as used (`Period.Month` is an identifier reference), so it is kept, but TypeScript has inlined the const enum and would normally drop the specifier. The rewritten declaration still imports `Period`, which has no runtime export.

Notes:
- The constructor parameter is not essential: any runtime value used only in type positions in the same statement triggers it, for example a field typed `PeriodFormatter | undefined`.
- Not needed: a barrel, tsconfig `paths`, or any non-default compiler option. `ngc` alone emits the file correctly.
- Possibly related: #34594 (22.2.6), which fixed type-only specifiers but not this case.
- Workarounds: a regular `enum`, or importing the const enum in its own import statement.

| @angular/* | @angular-devkit/build-angular | result |
| --- | --- | --- |
| 22.1.8 | 22.1.9 | builds |
| 22.2.0 | 22.1.9 | builds |
| 22.1.8 | 22.2.0 | fails |
| 22.2.0 | 22.2.0 | fails |
| 22.2.4 – 22.2.6 | 22.2.4 – 22.2.7 | fails |
| 22.2.7 | 22.2.8 | fails |
| 22.3.0 | 22.3.0 | fails |

